      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "```markdown\nThe formula to find the distance between two points $\\big(a, b\\big)$ and $\\big(a, c\\big)$ is given by:\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the starting points are $\\big(-3, -4\\big)$ and $\\big(4, -5\\big)$. Applying the formula:\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n$$\n\nSimplifying the terms inside the square root:\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2}\n$$\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{(7)^2 + (-1)^2}\n$$\n\nSquaring both terms:\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{49 + 1}\n$$\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{50}\n$$\n\nTaking the square root of 50 simplifies to:\n$$\nd = \\sqrt{25} \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n$$\nd = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt{2}\n$$\n\nThus, the distance between the two points is $\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}$ units.\n```",
