      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the distance between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a Cartesian plane, we use the distance formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nHere, the coordinates of the two points are \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\). We will substitute these values into the distance formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + ((-5) - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nNext, we simplify the expressions inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n4 - (-3) = 4 + 3 = 7\n\\]\n\\[\n(-5) - (-4) = -5 + 4 = -1\n\\]\n\nNow, we substitute these simplified values back into the distance formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the squares:\n\n\\[\n7^2 = 49\n\\]\n\\[\n(-1)^2 = 1\n\\]\n\nSo the expression becomes:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Distance} = \\sqrt{49 + 1} = \\sqrt{50}\n\\]\n\nFinally, we can simplify \\(\\sqrt{50}\\) by recognizing that 50 can be factored as 25 times 2:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\times 2} = \\sqrt{25} \\times \\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\n\\]",
